Chapter: RefusalArm
  cites Gpu chapter DeviceEffect

 The negative arm. This chapter must NOT produce a .wgsl file: `run.ps1` is
 required to exit 6 and name `CODEX_REFUSED_when_expression`, leaving whatever
 was at the output path untouched.

 It exists because the failure it guards against is invisible from the other
 side. A `when` used to lower to the literal `0`, so a kernel written with one
 compiled, validated, ran, and computed zero. Nothing in a green sweep can tell
 that from a kernel that was lowered correctly, which is why the check is a
 refusal at generation time rather than an assertion about the output.

 Run it as the paired control whenever BitOpsArm is run. A pass on the positive
 arm alone says the emitter emits; only this one says it still refuses.

 We say:

Section: Kernel

  ra-pick : Boolean, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  ra-pick (c) (a) (b) =
    when c
     is True -> a
     is otherwise -> b

  refusal-step : Integer, Integer, Integer -> [Device] Integer
  refusal-step (inb) (outb) (gid) = act
    v <- device-load inb gid
    device-store outb gid (ra-pick (v > 0) (v * 2) (0 - v))
  end

